WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Content Security Policy, or CSP, is one of the most powerful browser security headers.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.960
It is also one of the easiest to break when copied without understanding the application.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:24.060
CSP lets a site define which sources may load or execute resources such as scripts, styles, images, fonts, connections, frames, workers, and other browser-managed content.

4
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.980
A good CSP starts with inventory.

5
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:40.440
Which scripts are first-party, which scripts are third-party, which styles are needed, which image and font sources are legitimate, which API endpoints are called from the browser, which frames are expected, and which pages have different needs.

6
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.700
Without that inventory, the policy becomes guesswork.

7
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:51.700
CSP can reduce the impact of script injection and unsafe content loading when designed carefully.

8
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.280
Narrow sources are generally safer than casual wildcard policies.

9
00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:03.420
A policy that allows broad resource locations or many dynamic exceptions may look impressive while providing little practical constraint.

10
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.680
The point is not to make the longest policy; the point is to define the browser behavior the application actually needs.

11
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.320
Report-only rollout can help teams observe policy effects before enforcement.

12
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:21.420
In report-only mode, the browser can report what would have violated the policy without blocking the resource.

13
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:31.840
That gives teams a way to discover missing sources, legacy inline patterns, third-party dependencies, or pages that need a different policy before users are affected.

14
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:43.240
Inline scripts, third-party scripts, CDNs, analytics, tag managers, browser extensions, and legacy page templates complicate CSP.

15
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.340
These dependencies may be real business requirements, but they should be visible.

16
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.160
Each exception should have an owner, reason, and review path.

17
00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.340
A policy full of unexplained exceptions will be hard to maintain.

18
00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.260
CSP is defense in depth.

19
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:07.040
It does not replace output encoding, safe templating, input validation, or careful handling of untrusted data.

20
00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.940
Output encoding prevents unsafe data from becoming executable markup or script.

21
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.640
CSP constrains what the browser is allowed to load or execute if something unexpected reaches a page.

22
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:18.940
Both jobs matter.
